{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Great Places to Visit"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "The Galapagos Islands"}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "scholar > Wining and Dining > Complaint: What's in My Spinach?"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 15}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe routines and regular activities in a wider range of social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " The Present Perfect Tense vs. The Simple Past Tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Present Perfect tense to talk about a finished action or event that is connected to the present in some way. <br><br>You use the Simple Past tense to talk about a finished action or event <span class=\"strong\">not</span> necessarily connected to the present."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li>You can use the Present Perfect tense to talk about experience.</li><li> When you use the Present Perfect tense, you think about the past and present together. There is some connection between what happened in the past and now. </li></ul>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li>For example: </li><li><span class=\"italic\">I have eaten lunch.</span> = I ate lunch and now I am not hungry.</li><li><span class=\"italic\">He has bought a painting</span> = He bought the painting in the past and now he has a painting. </li><br><li>Look at the chart about how to form the Present Perfect tense:</li></ul>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use <span class=\"strong\"> have/has + the past participle</span>  of the verb in Present Perfect sentences."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Past tense form\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Past participle form\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"shelter\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"sheltered\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"sheltered\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"The Islands have sheltered wildlife.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"become\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"became\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"become\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They have become the final destination for many animals.\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "The Galapagos Islands have sheltered incredible wildlife for millions of years. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "It all began 3 million years ago."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Quantifiers"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use a quantifier before a noun to indicate the amount or number of an item. Quantifiers do not indicate a specific quantity but rather a general amount."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li>You use quantifiers with both count and non-count nouns.</li><li>Quantifiers like a <span class=\"italic\">few, several, many, hundreds, thousands</span>, or  <span class=\"italic\">millions</span>, can only be used with count nouns.</li><li>You can use all of the quantifiers below with both count and non-count nouns.</li></ul>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Quantifier\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Meaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"more<br><br> less/fewer<br><br> no/none<br><br> any /not any<br><br> some<br><br> a lot of / lots of<br><br> plenty of <br><br> enough\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"a larger number/amount <br><br> a smaller number/amount<br><br> zero, nothing<br><br> to ask about / say there is not<br><br> a small number/amount<br><br> a very large number/amount<br><br> more than you need<br><br> as much as you need\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Wind currents led many insects to the islands. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Several types of sea birds made their way to the Galapagos Islands."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to shelter "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to protect or shield from danger "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The islands have sheltered many animals."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob01aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear about natural wonders."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "wildlife "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "animals and plants found in their native habitats"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": " Example: The Galapagos Islands are home to a diverse wildlife."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob01aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "ancestor "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an early type of animal from which others have evolved "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: How did their ancestors arrive on the islands?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob01aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "current "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the strong motion of water or air "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Wind currents led many insects to the islands."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob01aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to glide "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " to move i