Galapagos Islands? <br><br>The Galapagos Islands have sheltered incredible wildlife for millions of years. <br>\nWhere did these animals come from? How did their ancestors arrive on the islands?"}, {"time": "12000", "image": "", "id": 1, "text": "It all began about 3 million years ago. Wind currents led many insects to the islands. Gulls and several other types of sea birds made their way there with their ability to glide over long distances.<br>\nSea lions and penguins swam there, with the help of the strong currents of the oceans.<br>\nMany other species arrived traveling on natural rafts, which originated along South American river banks.<br>\nIn fact, scientists believe that's how the famed Galapagos tortoises got there!<br>"}, {"time": "40000", "image": "", "id": 2, "text": "So, thanks to the wind and the sea and other natural phenomena, the Galapagos have become the final destination for the spiny-crested marine iguana, the lava lizard, the frigate bird, the pintail duck, and thousands of other species that have made the islands their home."}], "video_src": "engenn6le15ob01re2vid01"}}], "template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "video", "id": 2, "_data": []}]}]}